WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
The first film followed matter into sugar and wood.

2
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.200
Now ask how fast that building can go.

3
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.020
If light powers sugar-building, why can brighter light stop helping?

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.680
Place the same plant inside a clear chamber.

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.360
Its light dial starts low.

6
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.580
A sugar meter waits beside it.

7
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.640
We will change one condition at a time.

8
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:29.387
A rate tells how fast something happens.

9
00:00:30.587 --> 00:00:32.347
Here it tracks sugar made over time.

10
00:00:33.647 --> 00:00:35.367
Turn the light dial upward from dim.

11
00:00:36.527 --> 00:00:39.367
The gold beam brightens, and the green meter climbs.

12
00:00:40.787 --> 00:00:44.847
More light helps because light is holding the process back under these starting

13
00:00:44.847 --> 00:00:45.847
conditions.

14
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:48.157
Keep turning the light dial.

15
00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:52.657
The curve still rises, but each equal turn adds less.

16
00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:55.657
Soon it bends toward a level stretch.

17
00:00:56.857 --> 00:01:00.137
The beam brightens again while the sugar meter barely moves.

18
00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:04.977
Light is plentiful now, so more light is no longer the main help.

19
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:07.767
Predict before the next turn.

20
00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:13.227
If the light is already bright, will doubling it double the photosynthesis rate?

21
00:01:14.427 --> 00:01:15.627
Watch the curve and choose.

22
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:23.283
Here the rate changes little.

23
00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:26.763
Something else is now the tighter limit.

24
00:01:28.003 --> 00:01:30.483
A limiting condition holds a process back.

25
00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:35.443
When one condition becomes plentiful, a different one may matter more.

26
00:01:36.643 --> 00:01:40.823
The level curve reveals a limit, but it does not name that limit by itself.

27
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:44.283
Carbon dioxide can limit the rate.

28
00:01:45.723 --> 00:01:47.863
Raising it may help after light stops helping.

29
00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:51.103
Temperature also changes the rate.

30
00:01:52.343 --> 00:01:54.603
Warming can speed the process up to a point.

31
00:01:55.903 --> 00:01:57.283
Too much heat slows it down.

32
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:02.043
These conditions interact, so the important limit can change.

33
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:08.183
Light powers sugar-building from carbon dioxide and water; conditions limit its

34
00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:11.723
speed. This rule keeps two questions together.

35
00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:15.543
The first asks what supplies energy and matter.

36
00:02:16.763 --> 00:02:19.943
The second asks what controls the rate under these conditions.

37
00:02:21.443 --> 00:02:23.443
Answering one does not answer the other.

38
00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:27.427
Real blueberry leaves show the same useful shape.

39
00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:32.427
At low light, added light raised carbon dioxide uptake strongly.

40
00:02:33.747 --> 00:02:37.167
At high light, much more added light changed uptake only a little.

41
00:02:38.447 --> 00:02:41.487
Those measurements show where light stopped being the main limit.

42
00:02:42.707 --> 00:02:44.607
They do not identify the next limit.

43
00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:48.017
Use a curve as a clue, not a slogan.

44
00:02:49.357 --> 00:02:52.617
A steep rise says the changed condition mattered strongly.

45
00:02:53.917 --> 00:02:56.177
A level stretch says more of it helped little.

46
00:02:57.377 --> 00:03:00.657
Then test another condition while holding the others steady.

47
00:03:01.877 --> 00:03:05.557
That is how a simple chamber becomes a fair investigation.

48
00:03:06.497 --> 00:03:10.437
When more light stops helping, something else is holding the rate back.
